---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import RecipeCard from '../../../components/RecipeCard.astro'

const recipe = `name: billing-settings
source: file
file: captures/billing-settings.png
install: guide

marks:
  plan: { rect: [866, 874, 150, 50] }
  seats: { rect: [866, 948, 150, 50] }

callouts:
  - { mark: plan, text: The current plan, place: top }
  - { mark: seats, text: How many people it covers, place: bottom }`

const numbered = `name: billing-settings
source: file
file: captures/billing-settings.png

marks:
  plan: { rect: [866, 874, 150, 50] }
  seats: { rect: [866, 948, 150, 50] }

numbered: [plan, seats]`

const masked = `name: billing-settings
source: file
file: captures/billing-settings.png

mask:
  - { rect: [120, 96, 320, 40] }

marks:
  plan: { rect: [866, 874, 150, 50] }

callouts:
  - { mark: plan, text: The current plan }`
---

<Docs
  title="How to annotate an existing image"
  description="Draw shotlist callouts on a screenshot you already have, for screens no script can reach."
>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold">Annotate an existing image</h1>

  <p class="text-lg">
    Some screens cannot be reached by a script: a sign-in with a hardware key, a browser extension's
    own interface, a native dialog. This guide annotates a picture you took by hand, using the same
    callouts and the same publication step as every other recipe.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Step 1: put the image somewhere in the project</h2>

  <p>
    Take the screenshot however you like and save it in the repository. PNG, JPEG and WebP are all
    accepted, and the format is read from the file's contents rather than its name.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Step 2: measure the regions</h2>

  <p>
    There is no page to search, so marks are literal rectangles rather than queries. Open the image
    in any editor that shows pixel coordinates and note, for each region you want to point at, its
    <code>[x, y, width, height]</code> in image pixels.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Step 3: write the recipe</h2>

  <p>
    Set <code>source: file</code> and give <code>file</code> the path to the image, resolved from the
    configuration file's folder:
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={recipe} file="screenshots/recipes/billing-settings.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    Run it exactly as you would any other recipe. Callouts, numbered discs, style overrides,
    <code>install</code> and <code>--check</code> all behave the same way.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Number the regions instead</h2>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={numbered} file="screenshots/recipes/billing-settings.yaml" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Cover something in the image</h2>

  <p><code>mask</code> also takes literal rectangles here:</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={masked} file="screenshots/recipes/billing-settings.yaml" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Notes</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Playwright is still required. The callouts are drawn in a browser page, even though no site is
      opened.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>retries</code> has no effect on these recipes. There is no live page to be flaky, so a second
      attempt would reach the same answer.
    </li>
    <li>
      The coordinates are in the image's own pixels. If you re-take the picture at a different size,
      re-measure.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Related</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/reference/recipe#source">Recipe reference: <code>source</code></a></li>
    <li><a href="/docs/reference/callouts">Callouts and masks reference</a></li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/how-to/sign-in">Capture a page behind a sign-in</a>, when it can be automated
    </li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
